# The three-star question: an argument for the other side, with a reason and evidence someone could
# check. There is no key, so a grown-up marks it against the look-for.
item reading.cre-counter v=1 stars=3 skills=[reading.argument, writing.composition] {
  title "Argue the other side"
  let k=0..1

  scene 35x24 {
    sign note lines=[pick(k, "Ada: the wheel should turn,", "Mr Hale: close the bridge,"), pick(k, "because it can light the hall.", "because it is not safe.")] kind=board color=mint at=canvas(1, 1)
    text ask "Write a short argument against this: a claim, one reason, and one piece of evidence that someone could check." below=note gap=1 width=34
    writinglines lines lines=3 width=34 prompt="" below=ask gap=1
  }

  check writing.by-eye look-for="A claim that answers the one on the board (the wheel should stay still; the bridge should stay open), one reason that says why (it would cost too much to mend; people need the bridge to reach the kingfisher bank), and evidence that could be checked, such as a cost, a count or something seen (the axle is cracked; the ranger counted 120 crossings on a Saturday). The evidence may be made up for the exercise, but it must be the kind of thing someone could check. A child who writes only opinions has argued but not supported; ask how the council could find out it is true."
  hint "Start with the opposite claim. Why might someone want that?"
  hint "What could be counted, measured or seen that backs your reason?"
}
